{
 "thread": "U_W_33t60FA",
 "subject": "Help with dummy-coded interaction variables in a MIMIC model",
 "messages_listed": 4,
 "last_activity": "2020-09-14T12:53:49Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "CMt3Fa0aDAAJ",
   "from": "N Nuseibeh",
   "author": "9bd7054f8f855033",
   "date": "2020-09-08T14:02:45Z",
   "subject": "Help with dummy-coded interaction variables in a MIMIC model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>Hi everyone,</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m new to SEM and to Lavaan so apologies if this is a straightforward question! <span>My issue is with including (categorical, dummy-coded) interactions as predictors in a SEM model (I’m using Lavaan) and how to interpret the output. <br></span></div><div><span><br></span></div><div>The SEM model I&#39;m trying to build has 3 latent variables as below:<br></div><div><ul><li><span>Latent Variable 1: “Latent 1”</span><span><span></span></span><ul><li><span>Measured by 9 indicator items, scored between 1 (“strongly disagree”) and 5 (“strongly agree”)</span><span><span></span></span></li></ul></li><li><span>Latent Variable 2: “Latent 2”</span><span><span></span></span><ul><li><span>Measured by 15 indicator items, scored between 1 (“strongly disagree”) and 7 (“strongly agree”)</span><span><span></span></span></li></ul></li><li><span>Latent Variable 3: “Latent 3</span><span><span></span></span><ul><li><span>Measured by 8 Learner items , scored between 1 (“strongly disagree”) and 7 (“strongly agree”)</span></li></ul></li></ul></div><div><p><span>It has 5 main predictor variables (all categorical, therefore dummy-coded), as below:</span><span><span></span></span></p><ul><li><span>U Type (U1 = 1, U2 = 0)</span><span><span></span></span></li><li><span>Country (C1 = 1, C2 = 0)</span><span><span></span></span></li><li><span>SES (SESHigh = 1, SESLow = 0)</span><span><span></span></span></li><li><span>SSS  (SHigh = 1, SLow = 0)</span><span><span></span></span></li><li><span>F Type (F1 = 1, F2 = 0)</span></li></ul><div>And then I want to include interactions between these :<br></div><ul><li>U Type x Country</li><li>U Type x F Type</li><li>SES x F Type</li><li>Country x F Type</li><li>SSS x F Type</li></ul><div>My issue is how best to include these interactions so that I can interpret the results correctly. As it stands, I have included <span>included only ONE dummy picking up each 2x2 interaction, but then I&#39;m not sure how to interpret the results when that product term is significant. Any help would be really really appreciated!<br></span></div><div><br></div><div>My code for this is :<br></div><div><br></div></div><div><span style=\"font-family:Courier New\">library(semTools)<br>library(lavaan)<br>library(semPlot)<br><br>#data<br>data = MyData<br><br>#define MIMIC<br>MIMIC6 = &#39;<br><br>#Measurement_model<br>Latent1 =~ A1 + A2 + A3 + A4 + A5 + A6 + A7 + A8 + A9<br>Latent2=~ C1 + C2 + C3 + C4 + C5 + C6  + C7 + C8 + C9 + C10 + C11 + C12 + C13 + C14 + C15<br>Latent3 =~  L1 + L2 + L3 + L4 + L5 + L6 + L7 + L8<br><br>#Regress DV on group code IVs</span></div><div><span style=\"font-family:Courier New\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-family:Courier New\">Latent1 ~ ~ U1 + C1 + F1 + <span>SESHigh + SHigh + U1xC1 + U1xF1 + SESHighxF1 + C1xF1 + SHighxF1 </span><br><br>Consumer ~ U1 + C1 + F1 + <span>SESHigh + SHigh + U1xC1 + U1xF1 + SESHighxF1 + C1xF1 + SHighxF1 </span><br><br>Learner ~ ~ U1 + C1 + F1 + <span>SESHigh + SHigh + U1xC1 + U1xF1 + SESHighxF1 + C1xF1 + SHighxF1 </span><br>&#39;<br>#run the model<br>MIMIC6.fit = sem(MIMIC6,<br>                 missing = &quot;fiml&quot;,<br>                 data = MyData,<br>                 estimator = &quot;MLR&quot;)<br><br>#examine the model<br>summary(MIMIC6.fit,<br>        standardized = T,<br>        rsquare = T,<br>        fit.measures = T)</span><br><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "mo3zE8vrAgAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2020-09-11T08:40:38Z",
   "subject": "Re: Help with dummy-coded interaction variables in a MIMIC model",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div>My issue is how best to include these interactions so that I can interpret the results correctly. As it stands, I have included <span>included only ONE dummy picking up each 2x2 interaction</span></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>If you already calculated your product terms as new variables in the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">data.frame</font>, that is correct.  </div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"https://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/cat.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/cat.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792420756696000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2U-y_t84fVOE_tINTUM-m1\">https://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/cat.html</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>But you could also use the colon operator as a shortcut:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#606\">Latent1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> U1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> C1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> F1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">SESHigh</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">SHigh</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> U1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">C1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> U1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">SESHigh</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> C1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">SHigh</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">F1</span></div></code></div><br></div><div>FYI, you have an extra tilde after Latent1 and Learner.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div><span>then I&#39;m not sure how to interpret the results when that product term is significant. </span></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Probe the interaction.  </div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://quantpsy.org/interact/interactions.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://quantpsy.org/interact/interactions.htm&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792420756696000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3WjKTlA58tzjED_265DLuq\">http://quantpsy.org/interact/interactions.htm</a></div><div><br></div><div>You can <span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">label</span> the paths and calculate user-defined parameters that represent the conditional effects you want to test.  For example, if <span style=\"font-family:monospace;background-color:rgb(250,250,250);color:rgb(0,0,0)\">U1</span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;background-color:rgb(250,250,250);color:rgb(102,102,0)\">:</span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;background-color:rgb(250,250,250);color:rgb(0,0,0)\">C1</span> is significant, and you want to probe the effect of U1 for each level of C1:</div><div><br></div><div style=\"border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#606\">Latent1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\"><span style=\"color:#000\">b_u1</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">U1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\"><span style=\"color:#000\">b_c1</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">C1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> F1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">SESHigh</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">SHigh</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\"><span style=\"color:#000\">b_int</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">U1xC1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\">## user-defined parameters to probe interaction</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\"># </span><b><span style=\"color:#800\">b_u1 is already the effect of U1 when C1 == 0 (and F1 == 0)</span></b><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>b_u1_c1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b_u1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b_int </span><b><span style=\"color:#800\"># effect of U1 when C1 == 1</span></b><b><span style=\"color:#800\"> (and F1 == 0)</span></b></div></code></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792420756696000&amp;usg=AOvVaw15RkGXJE9vZuM3FQOCRF9G\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
